From a9eab736827af037903ca611476e92202c88ba55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:47:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: limited colour range made by the driver where
 the connector has Broadcast RGB

"Use limited colour range (16-235)" made Kodi draw everything in 16-235:
its GUI shaders, the video's shaders, the GUI composed over HDR, the DRM
PRIME renderer and the conversion of Dolby Vision to HDR10 all ask
UseLimitedColor(). The drivers with the "Broadcast RGB" property compress
the range themselves at "Automatic" in some modes:
- i915 and xe (intel_hdmi_limited_color_range()): limited range on every
  CTA mode of an HDMI sink but 640x480, whether the sink can select the
  range or not;
- amdgpu (Linux 7.3 with linux-9971 and linux-9983): limited range on the
  CTA modes of an HDMI sink without QS, full range on one with it.
With the setting on, the picture was compressed twice on Intel at every
TV mode (raised blacks, dull whites, as reported on the LibreELEC forum
with an Intel NUC11 and a calibrated LG C1), twice on AMD for a TV without
QS, and on both in YCbCr (HDR at 4K above 30 Hz, linux-9966 to 9972),
where the drivers make the limited range from the full range of the
framebuffer. On AMD with a TV with QS it went out in 16-235 while the AVI
InfoFrame said full range.

Where the connector has the property with "Limited 16:235" and
"Automatic", the setting now asks the driver for "Limited 16:235", and
UseLimitedColor() says no once the kernel has it: Kodi draws full range
and the driver compresses everything on the output once (the planes, the
cursor, the console after Kodi), with the AVI InfoFrame saying limited
range where the sink can be told. With the setting off the property is
not touched, but where Kodi itself left it at "Limited 16:235", which goes
back to "Automatic"; "Full" and "Automatic" are left as they are. The
decision is CRgbRange (RgbRange.h), for the host tests.

A change of the property needs a mode set (i915 makes one of it), so it
is staged only in a commit that allows one: Kodi's first, the one of a
change of mode, or one made for it when the setting changes while no
video plays. Never in a commit whose request carries the property already
(the transport of native Dolby Vision sets "Full" and puts back what it
found; the clean-up of a session that ended with it), nor while native
Dolby Vision has the output. A commit with it that the driver refuses is
asked again with the next mode set, twice in all: then Kodi compresses on
that output itself, as before, and the log says so. nvidia-drm has no such
property: Kodi compresses there, as before.

kodi.log says once for each mode and range what Kodi asks for, the
property and its value, and who compresses the range ("RGB range: ...");
on nvidia-drm, also what NVIDIA tells a display that can select the range
(full range) or one that cannot.
